which is really the the mechanism at the  at the core of all economies it's the  money creation mechanism  where does money come from  um and it's funny because when you study  economics as i've done  um i mean in any discipline whether it's  chemistry or physics or whatever you  know  you study it at the top universities you  expect  to become an expert in the important  stuff in your discipline  but when you study economics they don't  really teach you about  the money creation process and the  monetary mechanism  because the leading economic theories  and models as you know i mean they  i mean the the highbrow ones they they  don't even have any money whatsoever in  their model  um it's an abstracted um theoretical  economy where essentially people  exchange  things by a barter that's what they  assume  because money doesn't matter so we might  as well take it out of the equation it  just confuses things  i mean there's the famous book by david  romer  in fact i did a survey what's in in the  uk at british universities  what is the most widely used  masters level macroeconomics textbook  and it was david romer  you know it's his key book on  macroeconomics and growth  and and on page three it says um  you know essentially for more than half  in the in the book we  the models and theories don't include  money because this would only confuse  matters  we heard from jay powell just a few days  ago that it was no longer an appropriate  part it was no no longer an appropriate  mechanism for central banks to look at  the money supply and that is that is the  orthodoxy  yeah yeah yes indeed indeed but  but really it's it's surprising that he  says this now at this late stage because  certainly since  2008 a lot of people have realized that  oh  you know we neglected the monetary  mechanism  and and really um we can't just  exclude money uh we have to understand  how it works and where money comes from  and that was also my original puzzle  when i was shimomura fellow in tokyo in  1991 and i had this  this task to my research topic  uh on the basis of which i became the  first shimomura fellow was  to figure out japanese capital flows  that were buying up the world as you  started out mentioning you know japanese  money buying  pebbles golf course um rockefeller  center  columbia pictures whatever in australia  in hawaii and  in the uk they were buying up companies  real estate whatever  you name it um and it was just so much  money that  no standard economic model could explain  it all  the world's experts were saying we don't  know there is no answer  somebody told me oh there's this you  know famous u.s economist jeffrey  sachs he was recently in tokyo at  meaty the ministry of industry and trade  with his  i think former phd's and peter boone and  they've they've written a paper on just  this topic  richard you should go and speak to meaty  they will have the paper  and i went there met various bureaucrats  and they told me yes yeah  uh professor sax and peter boone they  were here oh yes we have their  discussion paper  which they presented and it was because  my fundamental idea was it it's clearly  linked to these  crazy asset prices land prices where the  imperial palace garden had as you said  the same value as the entire state of  california including los angeles san  francisco you name it  crazy um and so that there had to be a  link but what precisely was the link  there was the paper jeffrey sacks peter  boone  i was delighted because you know i was  just i barely finished my my first  degree at the lse  by the end of it i was very disappointed  to find there is no link that was their  conclusion  there is no link why the argument was  well okay you've got totally overpriced  japanese real estate  to the extent that it makes sense for  japanese land owners to just sell a  tiny plot of land and buy you know buy a  few big chunks of california why not  you know just for diversification  purpose alone would make sense and