sorts and then they  use some of those deposits and lend it  out so their financial intermediaries  are just in between the depositors  and the final use of the money  but it's not true but the conclusion is  this is not actually what happens  when a bank gives out a loan banks are  not financial intermediaries  um i could show this with data  but empirically i i approve this only  much later  this is published only in 2014  where did a separate empirical test this  is using  um german banking data because really  see  this financial intermediation theory is  only one of three theories of banking  and it's extraordinary i mean banking  has been around for five thousand years  you go back to  ancient babylonia and there were  essentially modern banks there was a  credit economy  um there was interest being charged  there were  collateralized loans uncollateralized  loans cashless transactions  you know it was a proper banking system  because essentially  banking is accounting and they kept good  records and we have them in the british  museum thousands and thousands of clay  tablets right  in cuneiform where they they note and  they're mostly accounting records and  banking records  so banking is ancient yet until  recently there's been a debate  still an ongoing debate a controversial  debate about  how banks actually work and there's  these three theories of banking now  the one that i described is the dominant  one the conventional one that's  in all the textbooks and finance journal  journal of finance and and so on  journal of uh money credit and banking  they think  banks are financial intermediaries this  is not based on  on empirical evidence by the way now  there's a second theory and that's the  older one  and um some people remember because that  used to be taught  in the textbooks also in economics  namely the fractional reserve theory of  banking  which says that well a bank always has  to  put a percentage um of the  of their deposits with the central bank  the fractional reserve and say it's  let's say  if it's one percent the bank gets a new  deposit of a hundred dollars  it will put one dollar with the central  bank and lend out 99 but the 99 will  then become somebody else's  some other banks new deposit when you  use it  and that bank will then put one percent  aside give it to the central bank as a  reserve and lend out  99 of that 98 point you know  and so on so the whole thing continues  that will then become  uh some other banks deposit each time  the amount gets smaller  and it's a geometric progression and  mathematically and you can calculate the  total  and with a one percent reserve  requirement the  100 initial deposit become 10  000 in aggregate in total deposits  the central bank has 100  as their total reserves from all the  banks these small amounts  and total new lending in the banking  system is 9  900. that's the fractional reserve  theory it's sort of  slightly complicated but sort of also i  mean there's a certain logic to it  um now that's not any more popular  as as you may know you may have noticed  i mean nowadays they  they don't talk about this they talk  only about this financial intermediate  theory banks are like non-banks there's  no  no difference now there's a third theory  and that one is the oldest that one was  popular from  essentially well until roughly the 1920s  1930s that's when the  fraction reserve theory started to  become dominant until the 60s  then we had the financial intermediation  theory and this oldest theory  is the credit creation theory of banking  now that's the  the most surprising one and that one  says  banks are not financial intermediaries  they do not intermediate they actually  creators of money  and when a bank gives out a loan  this is newly created money that's added  to the money supply but not  like in the fraction reserve story in  some kind of indirect collective  fashion where you can't kind of grasp  where's the money creation oh it's just 